<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sichuan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sichuan is a province of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, centred on a fertile basin. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> it was an important economic region where early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> developed. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> refer to military campaigns involving Sichuan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sichuan.jpg" alt="Sichuan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: chensiyuan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_huanglong_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in the 310s, and Huan Wen, the dominant general of the mid-century, conquered the Cheng Han state in <b>Sichuan</b> in 347, reached the outskirts of Chang'an against the Former Qin in 354 and retook Luoyang in 356, but was badly defeated by the Former Yan at Fangtou in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…oasis cities of the Tarim Basin in Eastern Turkestan, campaigns in Burma, Vietnam and Nepal, the suppression of rebellion on Taiwan and the two Jinchuan wars in <b>Sichuan</b>, and they extended the empire to its greatest extent of some 13 to 14.7 million square kilometres by various estimates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Contemporary cultures such as Sanxingdui in <b>Sichuan</b> show that the Shang were one of several Bronze Age centres in China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Paper money, from the jiaozi of <b>Sichuan</b>, issued by the state from 1024, to the huizi of the Southern Song, allowed transactions on a scale impossible with copper coinage alone, and helped to join regional markets into a national commercial system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chu was the main bridge between the Yellow River civilisation and the peoples of the Yangtze and the south, and by the third century BCE it had carried Zhou writing, ritual and statecraft across a territory stretching from the borders of <b>Sichuan</b> to the sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>King Huiwen's general Sima Cuo conquered Ba and Shu in 316 BCE, gaining the fertile <b>Sichuan</b> basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Military</a></li></ul></section>
